%S A400722 1,2,4,4,4,16,4,8,24,24,8,64,4,32,64,16,4,384,4,96,96,64,8,512,64,64,
%T A400722 128,128,32,3072,4,64,256,64,256,6144,16,64,128,384,16,6144,16,512,
%U A400722 3072,256,32,4096,16,4096,1024,512,32,8192,1536,1024,256,256,32
%N A400722 Number of divisors of (4^n - 1)/3.
%C A400722 Let I[x] denote the set of polynomials p = p(x) all of whose coefficients are integers. For p(x) in I[x], denote by f(x,i), for i=1..m, the irreducible divisors in I[x] of p(x). Let c be an integer.  The numbers f(c,i), together with 1, are here called type 1 (p,c)-divisors of p(c).  The products of two or more of the numbers f(c,i) that are divisors of p(c) are type 2 (p,c)-divisors of p(c); and all other divisors of p(c) are type 3 (p,c)-divisors. These definitions generalize naturally to polynomials in more than 1 variable. (See A398834 for a guide to related sequences and examples.)
%C A400722 For this sequence, the a(n) divisors of (4^n - 1)/3, for selected values of n are partitioned into the 3 types as shown below. Note that primes can occur in both types 1 or 3, or both, depending on n.
%C A400722 n    type 1        type 2              type 3
%C A400722 1    1             none                none
%C A400722 2    1,5           none                none
%C A400722 3    1,21          none                3,7
%C A400722 4    1,5,17        85                  none
%C A400722 5    1,341         none                11,31
%C A400722 6    1,5,13,21     65,105,273,1365     3,7,35,39,91,195,455
%F A400722 a(n) = A000005(A002450(n)).
%t A400722 {x, y} = {4, 1}; p[n_] := p[n] = (x^n - y^n)/(x - y);
%t A400722 Table[DivisorSigma[0, p[n]], {n, 1, 100}]
%Y A400722 Cf. A000005, A002450, A259178, A274906, A398834, A430723.
%K A400722 nonn,easy,new
